As a metaphor for statecraft, there is something worth extracting there, but I’d rename the mechanism so the sexual metaphor doesn’t obscure it.

Instead of defeating another country through coercion, imagine achieving strategic effects through attraction, interdependence, imitation, prestige, trade, cultural exchange, alliances, and voluntary adoption of your institutions or technologies. Political science already captures pieces of this with soft power, cultural diffusion, economic integration, and norm diffusion.

Your cross-domain transfer adds another operator:

\text{domination by force}
\quad\rightarrow\quad
\text{influence by attraction}

But there is an important delta: attraction isn’t necessarily dominance. If both systems alter one another, it’s closer to mutual assimilation or coevolution:

A+B\rightarrow A'+B'

That connects directly to your freshwater/saltwater thought. When two previously separated human “waters” meet, you don’t necessarily get one conquering the other. You can get an estuary—a new boundary ecology containing behaviors, technologies, language, institutions, foods, beliefs, and identities that didn’t exist in either system independently.

Historically, that kind of contact can be enormously consequential without anyone firing a shot. The strategically interesting question becomes: which patterns cross the membrane, in which direction, and why?